1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,367
Imagine you're paying online, and the moment
you tap Pay, an SMS arrives on your phone.

2
00:00:07,733 --> 00:00:10,833
It has a 6-digit code that's valid for only a
few minutes.

3
00:00:11,967 --> 00:00:15,233
You enter this OTP, and the payment is
confirmed instantly.

4
00:00:16,833 --> 00:00:21,933
But where is this code made, how does it reach your phone,
and how does the bank know the code is right?

5
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:27,867
Today, let's understand the technology behind
OTP, step by step.

6
00:00:30,733 --> 00:00:32,967
First, what exactly is an OTP?

7
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:37,667
OTP stands for One-Time Password, a password
that works only once.

8
00:00:40,233 --> 00:00:44,933
Your normal password stays the same for a long
time, but an OTP is new every time.

9
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:47,733
And it expires within a few minutes.

10
00:00:48,467 --> 00:00:54,233
OTPs are used for online payments, bank
logins, app sign-ups and password resets.

11
00:00:54,267 --> 00:00:56,767
It's considered part of two-factor
authentication.

12
00:00:57,467 --> 00:01:00,833
That means you need not just your password,
but your phone as well.

13
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:06,400
So even if someone learns your password, they
can't get in without the OTP.

14
00:01:08,533 --> 00:01:13,400
Many apps also send OTPs by email, but banks
mostly use SMS.

15
00:01:16,267 --> 00:01:20,133
To understand the OTP system, let's first look
at its main parts.

16
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:25,000
First is your app or website, where you're
logging in or paying.

17
00:01:25,267 --> 00:01:29,933
Second is the bank's or company's authentication
server, which creates and checks the OTP.

18
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,033
Third, the SMS gateway passes the message on.

19
00:01:34,867 --> 00:01:38,300
Fourth, your telecom operator's network
carries it to you.

20
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:43,000
And fifth is your phone, where the OTP
arrives.

21
00:01:43,067 --> 00:01:46,767
At every step in between, there's an effort to
keep the data secure.

22
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:49,367
Now let's go through these steps one by one.

23
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:54,333
So the question is, how is an OTP created?

24
00:01:54,367 --> 00:01:58,467
When you tap Pay or Login, the server creates
a random number.

25
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,433
It's a number that's very hard to predict in
advance.

26
00:02:03,967 --> 00:02:06,800
A secure random number generator is used for
this.

27
00:02:07,167 --> 00:02:11,533
An OTP is usually four to eight digits long,
and most often six.

28
00:02:11,967 --> 00:02:16,000
Six digits give one million possible
combinations, so guessing is very hard.

29
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:21,233
Then the server saves this OTP along with some
details.

30
00:02:21,767 --> 00:02:26,567
Like which user and which transaction this OTP
is for, and when it will expire.

31
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:30,967
Many systems don't store the OTP directly, but
store its hash instead.

32
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:36,733
A hash is like a digital fingerprint, and it's very
hard to get the original number back from it.

33
00:02:38,433 --> 00:02:43,000
So even if someone reaches the database, they
can't easily find the real OTP.

34
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:47,300
An OTP expires quickly, usually in five or ten
minutes.

35
00:02:50,467 --> 00:02:52,733
Now let's see how the OTP reaches your phone.

36
00:02:53,733 --> 00:02:57,133
The server sends the OTP message to an SMS
gateway.

37
00:02:57,467 --> 00:03:01,100
The SMS gateway passes this message to your
telecom operator's network.

38
00:03:02,033 --> 00:03:05,333
The operator's message center stores it and
delivers it to your phone.

39
00:03:06,667 --> 00:03:09,267
Your phone is online, so it arrives within
seconds.

40
00:03:11,433 --> 00:03:16,100
Banks use a registered sender ID in the message, so
you can recognise that the SMS is genuine.

41
00:03:18,033 --> 00:03:23,200
Many phones have OTP autofill, where the phone
reads the code from the SMS and fills it in.

42
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:26,933
Sometimes, when the network is busy, the OTP
can take a little longer.

43
00:03:27,767 --> 00:03:31,167
In roaming too, an OTP can sometimes take
longer to arrive.

44
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:36,300
That's why apps have a Resend OTP option,
which becomes active after a few seconds.

45
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:42,100
Now when you enter the OTP, what happens next?

46
00:03:44,167 --> 00:03:48,233
The app sends the OTP you entered to the
server over a secure connection.

47
00:03:48,867 --> 00:03:51,100
The server checks whether the OTP is correct.

48
00:03:52,167 --> 00:03:55,900
It also checks that the OTP hasn't expired and
hasn't been used before.

49
00:03:57,167 --> 00:04:00,200
In most systems, the number of wrong attempts
is also limited.

50
00:04:01,433 --> 00:04:06,133
After a few wrong attempts, the OTP may be
blocked, or you may have to wait a while.

51
00:04:07,767 --> 00:04:10,767
If all is correct, the server approves your
payment or login.

52
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:15,500
And once it's used, the same OTP won't work
again.

53
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:23,267
A payment OTP is tied to that one transaction,
so it won't work for any other payment.

54
00:04:24,433 --> 00:04:28,500
In card payments, this verification often
happens on your bank's secure page.

55
00:04:29,033 --> 00:04:32,200
In the same way, a login OTP is tied to that
login session.

56
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:39,500
Some OTPs don't come by SMS at all; an
authenticator app makes them.

57
00:04:40,567 --> 00:04:45,033
These apps make a new code every thirty seconds,
and they work even without the internet.

58
00:04:46,300 --> 00:04:50,000
This works with the TOTP method, a Time-based
One-Time Password.

59
00:04:51,767 --> 00:04:55,367
During setup, the website and your app share
one secret key.

60
00:04:56,567 --> 00:05:00,133
This secret key is usually saved in the app by
scanning a QR code.

61
00:05:00,633 --> 00:05:04,233
Then the app combines the secret key with the
current time to calculate a code.

62
00:05:05,300 --> 00:05:08,900
The server does the same calculation with the
same secret key and the same time.

63
00:05:09,633 --> 00:05:12,167
If both codes match, the login is approved.

64
00:05:13,867 --> 00:05:18,700
These codes depend on time, so your phone's
time setting must be correct.

65
00:05:18,867 --> 00:05:23,833
The server also accepts a small time difference, so a
small delay doesn't make the login fail.

66
00:05:24,267 --> 00:05:29,067
And it isn't tied to your SIM, so it's
considered safer against frauds like SIM swap.

67
00:05:31,133 --> 00:05:34,133
OTPs are very useful, but fraudsters target
them too.

68
00:05:35,333 --> 00:05:38,100
The most common trick is to call and ask for
your OTP.

69
00:05:38,467 --> 00:05:43,467
Someone claims to be from the bank or a KYC
team, and asks for your OTP.

70
00:05:43,767 --> 00:05:47,500
Remember, a bank never asks for your OTP by
call, or by message.

71
00:05:48,267 --> 00:05:52,967
The second trick is a fake link, where a fake
website gets you to enter your OTP.

72
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:57,700
The third trick is SIM swap, where a new SIM
for your number is obtained by deception.

73
00:05:57,833 --> 00:06:00,267
Then your OTPs go to the fraudster instead.

74
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:06,400
If your phone's network suddenly disappears for no clear
reason, contact your operator right away.

75
00:06:09,100 --> 00:06:12,433
Fraudsters can also see your OTP through
screen-sharing apps.

76
00:06:13,633 --> 00:06:17,400
So never install a remote-access app just
because a stranger asked you to.

77
00:06:18,333 --> 00:06:23,367
Some people even post fake customer care numbers online, so
always take numbers from the official website.

78
00:06:25,933 --> 00:06:27,600
Now a few simple safety tips.

79
00:06:27,833 --> 00:06:31,033
Never share your OTP with anyone, whoever they
may be.

80
00:06:32,700 --> 00:06:37,000
Read the OTP SMS carefully, and check the
amount and the merchant's name.

81
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:42,133
If you didn't start any transaction and an OTP
arrives, ignore it and tell your bank.

82
00:06:45,500 --> 00:06:50,167
Keep a screen lock on your phone, so no one
else can read the OTP shown in a notification.

83
00:06:50,867 --> 00:06:53,600
Always keep your registered mobile number
updated with your bank.

84
00:06:54,600 --> 00:06:59,367
Where the option exists, you can also turn on
authenticator-app two-factor for important accounts.

85
00:06:59,667 --> 00:07:05,000
And if fraud happens, tell your bank immediately
and call the Cyber Crime Helpline, 1930.

86
00:07:06,100 --> 00:07:08,500
And never turn off the alerts from your bank.

87
00:07:11,733 --> 00:07:14,667
In the future, even newer ways to log in are
coming.

88
00:07:14,700 --> 00:07:19,867
Like passkeys and biometric login, where
verification is done with a fingerprint or face.

89
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:24,633
With passkeys, it becomes very hard to trick
you into logging in on a fake website.

90
00:07:25,033 --> 00:07:28,567
But even today, OTP is an important layer of
security.

91
00:07:28,833 --> 00:07:33,433
So, to recap: the server creates a random OTP
and saves it with an expiry time.

92
00:07:35,033 --> 00:07:37,967
The gateway and the mobile network deliver it
to your phone.

93
00:07:39,167 --> 00:07:42,533
You enter the OTP, the server checks it, and
approves it.

94
00:07:44,133 --> 00:07:50,167
If you enjoy understanding the systems behind technology,
please like this video and subscribe to the channel.

95
00:07:51,233 --> 00:07:53,133
See you in the next video. Thank you!
